    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'If you\'re Rh-negative and your baby is Rh-positive, your body could form antibodies against your baby\'s blood type, which isn\'t a problem for this pregnancy but could affect a future one. The Anti-D injection, given around 26 to 28 weeks and again after delivery, prevents this from ever happening.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Your blood group includes an Rh factor, positive or negative, and if yours is negative while your baby\'s, inherited partly from the father, is positive, a small amount of mixing between your blood and your baby\'s, most likely during delivery, can trigger your immune system to start making antibodies. Those antibodies wouldn\'t harm this baby, but they\'d remain in your system and could attack a future Rh-positive baby\'s blood cells.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Your blood group is checked at your very first antenatal visit, and if you\'re Rh-negative, an antibody screen will also be done. The Anti-D injection is routine, preventive care, not a treatment for an existing problem, and it\'s considered safe for both you and your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor to confirm your blood group and Rh status if you\'re unsure, and when your Anti-D injection, if needed, is scheduled.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This is quiet, unglamorous prevention at its best, one routine injection protecting a pregnancy you haven\'t even had yet.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Rh-negative mothers with an Rh-positive baby can form antibodies affecting future pregnancies.',
        'This doesn\'t affect the current pregnancy directly.',
        'The Anti-D injection is given around 26 to 28 weeks, and again after delivery.',
        'It\'s routine, preventive care, not treatment for an existing issue.',
        'Considered safe for both mother and baby.',
      ]),
