    number: 244,
    month: 6,
    question: 'What should I do if my iron levels are found to be low?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 6 (weeks 23-27)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'If your iron or haemoglobin levels come back low, your doctor will usually start or adjust an oral iron supplement, alongside iron-rich foods in your diet. If levels are quite low, or oral iron isn\'t tolerated well, an intravenous (IV) iron option exists too, which corrects levels faster.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Oral iron is the standard first step and works well for most women, though it can cause constipation or an upset stomach for some, which is a common reason women stop taking it without telling their doctor. IV iron bypasses the digestive system entirely, which is why it\'s considered for more significant anaemia or when it\'s diagnosed later in pregnancy with less time to correct it through oral supplements alone.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Take your iron supplement consistently rather than skipping it due to side effects; mention any stomach discomfort to your doctor rather than simply stopping, since there are often ways to make it more tolerable. Pairing iron-rich foods, like leafy greens, jaggery, and dals, with vitamin C sources like citrus fruit can help your body absorb more from your diet too.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your doctor if you\'re struggling with side effects from your iron supplement, rather than stopping it on your own, and ask when your levels will be rechecked.',
