    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Yes, exercise is not only safe but genuinely recommended if you have gestational diabetes; it\'s one of the most effective, doctor-endorsed tools for keeping your blood sugar steady. Walking, swimming, stationary cycling, and prenatal yoga are commonly recommended options.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Movement helps your muscles take up glucose from your blood directly, which lowers blood sugar through a completely different pathway than insulin, making exercise and medication genuinely complementary rather than one replacing the other. Regular activity can even reduce how much insulin you need if you\'re on it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Aim for around 30 minutes of moderate activity most days, and a short walk specifically after meals is especially effective for managing the blood sugar rise that follows eating. If you\'re newly diagnosed and haven\'t been active, start gently and build up, and always check with your doctor before beginning anything new.',
