  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-058',
    number: 248,
    month: 6,
    question: 'Recognizing high or low blood sugar symptoms with gestational diabetes',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 6 onward, for those managing gestational diabetes',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Low blood sugar (hypoglycemia) can cause shakiness, sweating, sudden hunger, dizziness, or confusion, and needs prompt attention, usually a quick source of sugar followed by food. High blood sugar (hyperglycemia) tends to build more gradually, with increased thirst, frequent urination, and fatigue.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Low blood sugar happens most often if a meal is delayed or skipped, especially if you\'re on insulin, since your body has less circulating sugar than the treatment expects. High blood sugar builds when your diet, activity, or medication aren\'t quite matching what your body currently needs, which is exactly why home monitoring exists, to catch these patterns before they become extreme.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Keep a quick source of sugar, like glucose tablets or juice, on hand in case of a low blood sugar episode, and always follow it with a small snack once you feel better. Log your readings and any symptoms consistently, since patterns over time help your doctor fine-tune your plan far more than any single reading.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor if low blood sugar episodes happen often, or if your readings are consistently running high despite following your plan.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Learning your own body\'s signals here is genuinely empowering; most women get comfortable recognising and responding to these within a short time.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Low blood sugar: shakiness, sweating, hunger, dizziness, confusion.',
        'High blood sugar: increased thirst, frequent urination, fatigue.',
        'Low blood sugar often follows a delayed or skipped meal.',
        'Keep a quick sugar source on hand for low blood sugar episodes.',
        'Log readings and symptoms to help your doctor fine-tune your plan.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
